---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { BUY_URL, PRICE, FOUNDING_PRICE, FOUNDING_CODE } from "../lib/product.mjs";
---

<Layout
  title="DiskPrune vs CleanMyMac — $19 lifetime, Trash only"
  description="CleanMyMac is a subscription maintenance suite. DiskPrune is a $19 lifetime Mac storage instrument that only moves what you approve to Trash."
>
  <main class="prose-page mx-auto max-w-prose px-6 py-16">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted"><a href="/">DiskPrune</a> · Compare</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight">
      DiskPrune vs CleanMyMac
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted leading-relaxed">
      They are not the same category. CleanMyMac is a paid subscription suite:
      malware scans, updaters, and a cleaner that markets large “cleaned”
      numbers. DiskPrune is a native storage instrument. Scan is free. Cleanup
      is a {PRICE} lifetime license. The only mutation is moving approved items
      to Trash.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Price</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
      DiskPrune is {PRICE} once, up to three Macs you own or control, 14-day
      refund. CleanMyMac is a subscription (and often a Mac App Store upsell).
      If you want an antivirus-plus-updater-plus-cleaner bundle, that is their
      product. If you want to understand why the volume is full and then trash
      specific known locations, paying yearly for a suite is the wrong shape.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What happens to files</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
      DiskPrune cannot permanently delete. There is no removeItem fallback, no
      empty-Trash call, no snapshot deletion. A dry-run sheet says “Nothing has
      changed yet.” The receipt reports estimated recoverable, moved to Trash,
      and storage immediately available as three different numbers — because
      they are.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
      CleanMyMac’s marketing treats cleanup as a cleaned-gigabytes event.
      That is a different trust model. We will not race them on a bigger number.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">System Data, Xcode, Docker</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
      DiskPrune names Xcode DerivedData, protects Docker.raw, and lists APFS
      snapshots without a delete control. Those three are the usual “System Data
      is huge” story. A suite that offers to sweep them in one click is offering
      to be wrong in a way you cannot undo.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
      Read <a href="/blog/clear-system-data-mac">System Data</a>,
      <a href="/blog/how-to-delete-xcode-deriveddata">DerivedData</a>, and
      <a href="/blog/shrink-docker-disk-mac">Docker.raw</a> if that is the actual
      problem on your Mac.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Notarization</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
      Both ship notarized. DiskPrune is a Developer ID signed, notarized, and
      stapled DMG — it opens with a double-click. <a href="/download">Download it</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-6" style="border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink">Scan free. {FOUNDING_PRICE} founding lifetime with code <span class="coupon">{FOUNDING_CODE}</span>. Trash only.</p>
      <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={BUY_URL}>Buy DiskPrune — {FOUNDING_PRICE} <span class="opacity-60 line-through ml-1">{PRICE}</span></a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/mac-disk-space-analyzer">Full comparison table</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
